Mercury's TCP listeners bound to the loopback. Mercury 1.9.24 binds its control port and its ARQ data port (open_listener_socket, used for both) and its KISS broadcast port
(tcp_server_thread) on INADDR_ANY, every network interface, and has no bind option on its command line or in its configuration file; nothing on those ports asks for a
password (1.9.24 takes one host at a time on the control and data ports, but the first to connect is any host). Any host on the LAN could write `TUNE -15` to the control port and key the transmitter for a minute, or hand a KISS frame to the broadcast port and have it sent on HF, past
every interlock of NeoAPRS (the page's access key, RX only, the call). NeoAPRS reaches the Mercury it starts on this computer alone, so the two binds become INADDR_LOOPBACK. The code is the same
on every system (Winsock has the same bind and the same constant): the patch is applied to the build for Linux, macOS and Windows alike, and there is no firewall rule to stand in for it. The third place
Mercury binds a socket, the websocket of its UI interface and web page (ui_transport_init, off unless the ini says ui_enabled or Mercury is started with -G: NeoAPRS turns it on for the spectrum scope, the transmit level
and the channel-busy control), is given the loopback as well, so that no listener of the program is left that the network could reach.
scripts/build-mercury applies this file to the pinned source before the build (`patch -p1`) and checks that no INADDR_ANY is left in the listeners' file; the daemon checks at every start that the
program it started listens on 127.0.0.1 only (neoaprs/mercuryhelper.py, `foreign_listeners`), and the release workflow starts the built program on each system and reads that table of sockets. A bind option has
been asked of upstream; when a Mercury carries one, this file goes and the option takes its place on the command line. Made from the source of the pinned commit (neoaprs/mercury.py: SOURCE_COMMIT); a new pin
that moves these lines fails the build loudly, which is right.

--- a/data_interfaces/tcp_interfaces.c
+++ b/data_interfaces/tcp_interfaces.c
@@ -277,7 +277,7 @@
 
     memset(&local_addr, 0, sizeof(local_addr));
     local_addr.sin_family = AF_INET;
-    local_addr.sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY;
+    local_addr.sin_addr.s_addr = htonl(INADDR_LOOPBACK);
     local_addr.sin_port = htons(port);
 
     if (bind(fd, (struct sockaddr *)&local_addr, sizeof(local_addr)) < 0)
@@ -2005,7 +2005,7 @@
 
     memset(&local_addr, 0, sizeof(local_addr));
     local_addr.sin_family = AF_INET;
-    local_addr.sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY;
+    local_addr.sin_addr.s_addr = htonl(INADDR_LOOPBACK);
     local_addr.sin_port = htons(tcp_port);
 
     if (bind(tcp_socket, (struct sockaddr *)&local_addr, sizeof(local_addr)) < 0)
--- a/gui_interface/websocket/mercury_websocket.c
+++ b/gui_interface/websocket/mercury_websocket.c
@@ -128,7 +128,7 @@
 
     memset(&opts, 0, sizeof(opts));
     opts.port = port;
-    opts.bind_addr = NULL;                      /* 0.0.0.0, as before */
+    opts.bind_addr = "127.0.0.1";               /* the loopback only */
     opts.ws_path = "/websocket";
     opts.max_message_size = UI_MAX_MESSAGE_SIZE;
     opts.tls_enabled = tls_enabled;
